The signals show two "Contact us" links with **different raw hrefs** (page1.html vs page3.html). Per the rubric I must not clear on raw-href equality alone. I'll resolve both destinations now.

resolve_destination(["/html/body/p[1]/a[1]", "/html/body/p[1]/a[2]"])

{"verdict":"REPRODUCED","confidence":"high","summary":"Two links share the identical accessible name 'Contact us' yet resolve to different destinations (page1.html vs page3.html), making their individual purposes indistinguishable.","reasoning":"The deterministic signals confirm distinctRawHrefs=2 for the same name 'Contact us'; resolving destinations confirms page1.html ≠ page3.html, satisfying the identical-names-different-destination failure mode under 2.4.4 — neither the name alone nor the enclosing block text ('Learn more (Contact us) and get in touch (Contact us)') disambiguates which 'Contact us' goes where.","evidenceRefs":["sameNameLinks.peers[0].href=page1.html vs rawElementHtml href=page3.html","enclosingHtml shows both links named 'Contact us' in the same paragraph with no per-link distinguishing context","rubric: identical names to DIFFERENT destinations is a barrier"]}

